one further down on the faisal Farook  works as a customer services assistant  for Virgin Trains he joined the company  after deciding to turn his life around  while in prison just before my release  version came in to prison to do  interviews I was one of the lucky few to  get an interview after the my interview  I was released I was told the Virgin  would contact us two months after I was  released I was working for virgin and  since then of ever since been working  for I'm not left the company has been  proactively recruiting people with  convictions for five years and has  banned the criminal record declaration  form on job applications around one in  six people in Britain has a criminal  record of those who've been to prison  75% have no job lined up when they come  out new research suggests that asking  people to declare convictions upfront  may be putting them off applying while  offering employers a false sense of  security  Adriana pace runs a campaign group that  works to get ex-offenders back into  employment for many people I guess is a  question of despair because they know  that were they to tick that box that's  regardless of their skills and  experience they would be immediately  discounted from the role and that is  very very frustrating when you're  encountering that day after day after  day when all you want is the opportunity  to gain employment to then be able to  sustain that employment and to rebuild  your life in a statement the Ministry of  Justice said criminal record  arrangements need to strike the right  balance between rehabilitation and  public protection and the current  measures do just that all cautions and  most convictions become spent over a  specified length of time after which  offenders are not required to disclose  their convictions employers say they  need to manage the risk in recruitment  but some feel this is best done in  person they often people with a previous  conviction turn out to be very good  employees they're looking to actually  turn around lives and will may be giving  an extra bit to the employer obviously  it depends on the nature of the crime  that has been  committed I think that's a discussion  that the employer and the interviewee  should have on a face to face discussion  employment reduces the rate of  reoffending by 60% and with reoffending  costing the state 13 billion pounds a  year campaigners say it's time to start  looking beyond an applicant's past and a  Brady Sky News
 

